<section>
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:780px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">What's usually sealed or restricted</h2>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px"><strong>The Presentence Investigation Report (PSR)</strong> is the most common example. It's prepared by U.S. Probation for the sentencing judge and is not publicly filed on the docket — access is generally limited to the parties, their counsel, and the court. A record-retrieval service that promises to pull a sealed PSR from PACER is promising something PACER access does not provide.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8">Grand jury materials, certain sealed motions, and some exhibits can also be restricted depending on the case. If a case is largely sealed, that's worth knowing before paying for retrieval, not after.</p>

    <h2>Why the order matters</h2>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px">A § 2255 motion, a § 2241 petition, and a compassionate release motion are all built out of this same material, read together — the charging document next to the plea agreement next to the sentencing transcript. Missing one piece is how a motion gets built on an assumption instead of a fact.</p>
    <p style="font-size:16px;color:#39414B;line-height:1.8">This page describes what generally exists in a federal case file. It isn't a review of your case, and it isn't legal advice — for that, see a <a href="/#contact" style="text-decoration:underline">free case review</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

